I just noticed something peculiar in my most recent game, and I'm
pretty sure it's a bug. I don't see anything about it on the official
bug list(s), so I'd like to check here before submitting a bug report.

Assume that the player observes (by sight or by feel) one or more
objects sitting on the floor of the dungeon in a lit area and then
moves out of sight of those objects. The map will show those objects
in their last known position. If the player becomes blind and
levitates, the game handles this in an appropriate manner. When the
player levitates over the square containing the objects, (s)he can
neither feel nor see them. Whether or not the objects are still there,
the map will continue to show the objects in their original position.
Similarly, if the objects have moved, the player will not discover
them by levitating blindly over their new position. This makes sense
-- even though the player didn't see or feel the objects as he passed
over their last known position, he has no reason to believe that
they've moved.

Here's the bug:

If the objects reside on a darkened square, a player levitating over
that square while blind will suddenly "discover" that the objects
aren't there. This happens even if, in fact, they are. I haven't
tested every type of object, but so far it seems to work with
anything.

Since the player is blind, this should be handled the same way
regardless of how the room is lit.

Am I correct in thinking that this is a bug, or is there an
intentional logic to this that I've missed?

I'm using the official win32 3.4.3 binaries.

I think this may be related to:
C343-32 (Fixed) - "When blind and levitating, the hero can still
discover stairs."
SC343-13 (Open) - "While blind and levitating, too much information
may be presented."

SC343-13 sounds similar, but I don't think it's describing the same
bug. It isn't so much that the game is giving too much information as
it is that the game gives bogus information. The description is rather
vague, though, so it might be referring to this issue.


